# Small-Team Git Workflows

This directory defines provider-neutral workflows for one to five
write-capable human maintainers. It grants no permission. A workflow may be
used only after a fresh, non-conflicting collaboration assessment and a
project-owned accepted decision select it. Tool availability, a collaborator
count, a workflow record, or a branch name is not authority to edit, publish,
review, merge, delete, or release anything.

The closed machine-readable portfolio is
`small-team-git.json`. Its operation IDs resolve through
`.agent/tools.json`; unknown operations fail closed. `pull` and raw
`force_push` are prohibited because they hide integration or bypass a known
remote revision. External publication, hosted changes, exceptional history
replacement, destructive recovery, and releases retain their distinct current
authority requirements.

## Selection boundary

Team topology selects only a base collaboration workflow:

| Assessed topology | Base workflow |
|---|---|
| One write-capable human, no expected concurrent writers | `solo_direct` |
| One write-capable human seeking reviewable integration | `solo_hybrid` |
| Two write-capable humans | `pair_pr` |
| Three to five write-capable humans | `tiny_pr` |
| More than five write-capable humans | `unsupported_team_size` |

Unknown, stale, or conflicting topology selects nothing. Read-only access and
bots do not increase the human-maintainer count. Activity does not erase
dormant write authority. Expected simultaneous human or agent work adds only
the `concurrent_work` modifier; agents do not increase the human team band.

Risk, external effects, and assurance remain separate axes. They may add
self-review, qualified review, stronger validation, protected execution, or an
external-authority gate without changing the team band. Team size alone never
selects Standard or High Assurance.

## `solo_direct`

Use only for one writer with no expected concurrent writers. Inspect the
working tree and exact revision, explicitly fetch and fast-forward when remote
state must be observed, stop on divergence, make one bounded change, run local
validation, stage exact reviewed paths, commit, recheck remote state, and
publish only with current authority. Observe CI after publication. A failed CI
run leaves the work incomplete; fix forward or create a validated revert
rather than replace published default-branch history. Substantial or risky
work escalates to `solo_hybrid` through a project-owned decision or current
direction within existing authority.

## `solo_hybrid`

Refresh the declared base, create a short-lived task branch, make and validate
the bounded change, stage exact paths, commit, and optionally publish a
self-PR. Observe CI and record a self-review that states its lack of
independence and remaining test gaps. Select exactly one integration method
during project adoption, resolve only understood in-scope conflicts, validate
again after resolution, integrate, and remove only fully integrated local or
explicitly authorized remote branches. A self-PR does not create peer approval
or merge authority.

## `pair_pr`

Use a short-lived task branch and PR. Local validation precedes publication;
CI and one peer review apply when an eligible independent reviewer is
available. If established review capacity is temporarily unavailable, the
workflow blocks. A project-specific exception, if separately authorized,
must be time-bounded and recorded as an exception rather than relabeled peer
review. Findings or failed CI return the work to implementation. Adopt exactly
one integration method and clean branches only after successful integration.

## `tiny_pr`

Use the same branch, PR, CI, finding loop, integration, and cleanup discipline
as `pair_pr`. Require at most one peer approval. Lightweight subsystem
ownership may guide reviewer selection when the project has evidence that it
helps, but it must not become a multi-level ownership or approval hierarchy.

## Integration and conflict handling

Project adoption selects exactly one of `merge_commit`, `squash_merge`, or
`rebase_then_fast_forward` for each adopted branch/PR workflow. Do not enable
several methods and choose opportunistically. Rebase replaces history: it
requires exact current authority, a non-default short-lived branch, and a
named upstream. Rebase before publication when practical. Replacing already
published task-branch history additionally requires an exact expected remote
object lease and `force_push_with_lease`; raw force-push and default-branch
history replacement are prohibited.

On a conflict, identify the exact files and declared write scope. Resolve only
understood in-scope changes and rerun relevant validation. Abort and block when
the conflict is unrelated, ownership is unclear, or correctness cannot be
established. Never discard another worker's changes to make integration pass.

## Concurrent-work modifier

When humans or agents may write simultaneously, use a branch or worktree per
task, record the shared base revision and declared write scope, detect overlap
before integration, and define cancellation, handback, and partial-result
rules. Remove only an exact clean worktree after handback. Coordination
metadata prevents silent collisions; it creates no permission.

## Governed work completion

All four base workflows and the `concurrent_work` modifier use the one shared
`.agent/scripts/octon_work_completion.py` engine. Assurance profiles use that same engine;
they may add checks, evidence, specialist review, protected enforcement, or
coordination controls without selecting another implementation.

The interface is:

```text
./octon work finish plan
./octon work finish apply --accept-digest <digest> --authorization-file <current-attestation.json>
./octon work finish resume [--receipt-id <WCR-id>] [--authorization-file <renewed-attestation.json>]
```

`plan` reads the accepted workflow decision, completed task, explicit task
completion contract, project hook assessments, local Git state, and locally
available remote-tracking refs. It prints a deterministic plan and performs no
write, fetch, hosted query, generated refresh, receipt update, or external
action. The optional completion-event hook may invoke only this plan command.
It is disabled by default.

`apply` reconstructs the plan, requires its exact reviewed digest, rechecks
the local preconditions, and requires a current task-scoped authorization
attestation covering the exact planned network and hosted operations. The
attestation records independently granted authority; it is not itself a grant.
Configuration, workflow adoption, command invocation, a template, or a prior
authorization for another plan is insufficient.

The engine stages only `work_completion.eligible_staging_paths` recorded on
the completed task plus the exact current `work.close` receipt and its
unchanged dirty postimages. It rejects every other pre-staged or different
dirty inventory and runs
only configured `read_only` project hooks in ephemeral `--observe-only` mode,
requires no active Git hooks or `core.fsmonitor`, and rejects empty or
duplicate commits. It re-observes remote and hosted state
immediately before material actions. PR workflows reject draft, conflicted,
ambiguous, mismatched, or stale PRs; checks and reviews must apply to the exact
head revision. `pair_pr` and `tiny_pr` observe one approval by an explicitly
configured eligible provider identity other than the author and never submit
or fabricate that approval. `solo_hybrid`
records self-review hashes and the lack of independence.

External actions are not atomically reversible. Progress is therefore
monotonic and resumable. Local receipts live under the repository Git common
directory at `octon-mini/work-completion/receipts/`, outside the source
worktree, so a receipt does not contaminate the commit or prevent cleanup.
Each receipt records the reviewed plan, exact revisions, authorization
evidence, hosted observations, completed operations, block reason, and a safe
resume command. Apply recognizes an already completed commit, publication,
PR, integration, synchronization, or deletion before acting, so retry does not
duplicate it. These local receipts are nonportable progress evidence until a
project explicitly exports them through its own evidence process.

Repository-local lifecycle transaction evidence remains visible. A close
plan written under `.agent/transactions/plans/` must be declared as an exact
task staging path; only the current matching close receipt/postimages are
derived automatically. Older, modified, ambiguous, or unrelated transaction
artifacts block rather than being excluded.

Cleanup is ordered: prove integration, remove the exact remote task branch
when adopted and authorized, fast-forward the local default branch to the
observed remote revision, then remove the exact non-current local task branch
only when no other worktree owns it. A missing, unknown, stale, divergent, or
contradictory fact blocks and leaves the completed evidence intact.

To enable the capability, a project owner explicitly updates
`.agent/project.json` through the normal reviewed transaction process. Set
`work_completion.status` to `enabled`; record repository identity, remote and
default branch; assess the provider, optional solo self-PR, required check
names, eligible provider reviewer identities for peer workflows, read-only
validation hooks, the `require_none` Git-hook policy, and required local/remote
cleanup for branch workflows.
Explicitly configure the existing `commands.work_completion_plan` hook to the
exact read-only `./octon work finish plan` argv; then optionally set the completion
event to `plan_only_on_completion_event`. Leaving `status` and the
hook `disabled` makes no change to normal project operation. Existing
snapshots obtain the engine only through an explicit Octon Mini upgrade or
package update; neither operation silently changes accepted decisions,
workflow adoption, hooks, providers, branch rules, or authorization policy.
An installed exact-pristine portfolio can be updated from Octon Mini source
with `./octon maintain package plan --package small-team-git-portfolio --update`
and the ordinary reviewed digest/apply flow. Modified package paths block the
update for manual reconciliation.

With the event enabled, successful application of a `work.close` transaction
dispatches and prints only the read-only plan. A post-closure planning block is
reported without undoing the truthful closure receipt and without starting
apply, publication, integration, or cleanup.

Planning, structural validation, a commit, an open PR, or a completed receipt
does not prove release, production, product-efficacy, or commercial readiness.

## Deliberately unsupported workflows

The portfolio does not support GitFlow, merge queues, release trains,
stacked-PR dependency trains, fork-first internal contribution, multi-level
CODEOWNERS approval, multiple mandatory approval stages, dedicated release
manager handoffs, organization-wide ruleset orchestration, multi-environment
promotion pipelines, or enterprise issue/portfolio governance. Simple CI,
PRs, one-peer review, and project-appropriate branch protection remain valid
small-team controls.

See `github-adapter.md` only when a project is considering GitHub. That guide
is an inactive example and does not assert any hosted configuration.
